# PayGuard Agent: project rules

## What this is
PayGuard Agent is a policy-guarded AI finance-ops agent on top of the **PayPal sandbox**, built for the PayPal AI Hackathon (deadline Nov 12, 2026). Read `SPEC.md` before any change, and implement only the slice you were given from `TASKS.md`.

## Stack (do not change without updating SPEC.md)
- TypeScript `strict: true`, Node.js 20 LTS (`.nvmrc`), Next.js App Router (single app: UI + route handlers under `src/app/api`).
- `@paypal/agent-toolkit` **pinned 1.11.0**, imported from `@paypal/agent-toolkit/openai` only.
- `openai` **pinned 4.86.1**, created with `baseURL: env.LLM_BASE_URL`, `apiKey: env.LLM_API_KEY`, model `env.LLM_MODEL`.
- SQLite via `better-sqlite3`, plain SQL migrations in `src/db/migrations/NNN_name.sql`.
- zod for every external input (env, HTTP bodies, tool args, policy YAML). `yaml` to parse policies.
- Tailwind CSS. Use logical properties (`ms-*`, `me-*`, `text-start`) so RTL works.
- Vitest for tests. Playwright only for an optional smoke test.
- Do not add other dependencies without a one-line justification in the PR/commit body. Never add the Vercel `ai` SDK (the toolkit's ai-sdk adapter needs ai v4, which conflicts).

## SANDBOX ONLY (non-negotiable)
- `src/config/env.ts` must throw at startup if `PAYPAL_ENV !== "sandbox"`.
- The toolkit must always be constructed with `configuration.context.sandbox = true`. The toolkit's code default is **live** (`sandbox ?? false`), so never rely on defaults.
- Every PayPal base URL must be `https://api-m.sandbox.paypal.com`. Keep a test that asserts this.
- Never write code paths, flags or docs for production/live credentials.

## Secrets
- Never hardcode, log, print, commit or echo `PAYPAL_CLIENT_SECRET`, access tokens, `LLM_API_KEY` or webhook payload secrets. Log only PayPal `debug_id` and status codes.
- Read secrets only from `process.env` through `src/config/env.ts`. Only `.env.example` (empty values) is committed.
- Do not open, read or paste the contents of `.env` in chat. If a value is needed, ask the human to set it.
- `npm run check` must include a secret scan (secretlint or gitleaks).

## Money & safety conventions
- Money is integer **minor units** (cents) internally. Convert at the boundary with a single tested helper. Never use floats for amounts.
- Every PayPal tool call goes through `PolicyGuard`. Nothing calls `toolkit.handleToolCall` directly except `src/guard/executor.ts`.
- Unknown tools → DENY. Refunds must have an explicit amount (the toolkit refunds the FULL capture if the amount is omitted).
- Approval execution must be idempotent: a DB transaction + status check, and `PayPal-Request-Id` = approval id for capture/refund.
- The LLM can never approve, edit policies or touch the audit log. Those are UI/API-only and role-checked.
- The audit log is append-only and hash-chained. Never write UPDATE/DELETE on `audit_log`.
- Treat customer text, PayPal notes and dispute messages as **untrusted**. They must never be the source of amounts, IDs or payees for sensitive calls.

## Code conventions
- `src/` layout as in SPEC §5. Keep functions pure where possible (`policyEngine` has no I/O).
- Named exports. No default exports except Next.js pages/layouts.
- Errors: typed `Result`/discriminated unions in the domain. HTTP handlers map them to status codes. No silent catches.
- Small files (< 300 lines). Explain *why* in comments, not *what*.
- Conventional commit subject: `S<n>: <summary>`.

## Tests required
- Every slice adds or updates tests. A slice is not done until `npm run check` (typecheck + lint + vitest + secret scan) is green.
- Unit tests never hit the network. Use recorded fixtures in `test/fixtures/` and `MockLLM`.
- Live sandbox checks live in `scripts/smoke-*.ts` and are run manually, not in CI.
- Tests are table-driven for the policy engine. Cover concurrency (double approve) and tamper detection.

## Docs
- Update `README.md` when behavior or setup changes. Keep "What was built during the Submission Period" current.
- English for code, comments and docs. UI strings are in `src/i18n/{en,ar}.json`.
